## Units

| # | Year | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Processes, anchors and works | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 6 | How people live, what pictures push. *What can artworks tell us about how people live and what they value, and how do an image's parts push a viewer to think, feel or act?* | 16 | va-g6-look; va-g6-craft | VA:Re7.1.6a; VA:Re7.2.6a; VA:Cr2.1.6a; VA:Cr2.2.6a | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2); connecting touched through each work's setting. Works: Jacob Lawrence, The Migration Series (United States, 1940 to 1941; the panels are shared between The Phillips Collection, Washington, and the Museum of Modern Art, New York), the unit plan picks two panels on travel and work, described and linked; a kente cloth woven by Asante weavers (Ghana, twentieth century; the unit plan picks one from an open-access collection, with its pattern names and occasions of use); posters of the federal Work Projects Administration (United States, 1936 to 1943, the Library of Congress poster collection, public domain), made to move people to act | Retrieval in day 2's opener: comparing readings and finding cultural links (va-g5-look) and the practice log and care of tools (va-g5-craft). VA:Re7.1.6a: interpret works from more than one part of the world for how people live and what they value (Lawrence's panels: people moving north for work and safety; the kente: patterns named for sayings and worn on set occasions), in a written look-describe-interpret response. VA:Re7.2.6a: mark the visual parts (value contrast, color, placement, lettering) and the cultural ties in two posters that steer a viewer, and say what each wanted people to do. VA:Cr2.1.6a: show openness to new materials and methods: a material test sheet tries charcoal, ink wash and chalk pastel, each with a log line on what surprised, and one is carried into the still life. VA:Cr2.2.6a: explain how saving, caring for and cleaning supplies affects the environment (rinse water, paper scraps, dried paint, reuse), from a one-week studio waste audit and the change the class makes. Technique: value in graphite, a ten-step value scale, hatching, cross-hatching, stippling and blending, applied to a still life drawn from observation. Words: value, contrast, interpret, persuade, conserve, reuse. Showing: the value still lifes with one-line notes in a hallway display. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Re7.1.6a, a written item: an unseen open-licensed work from another part of the world, what it shows about how people live and what they value, with two details as evidence; VA:Re7.2.6a, a written item: an unseen public domain poster, its visual parts marked and what it wants a viewer to think, feel or do; VA:Cr2.1.6a, a guide look-for on the kept material test sheet and log: three new materials tried, a log line each, one carried into the still life; VA:Cr2.2.6a, a kept card made on the last making day: the waste audit with the student's explanation of one environmental effect of saving, care or clean-up and the change made. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: persistence over many sessions (grade 7), copyright (grade 8). Tools: step cards for value and the three materials, the waste-audit sheet, work cards and poster cards, all Curio Class's own; a value-scale practice tool or its printed strip; a work viewer on the teacher screen or the printed card. Free path: two pencils of different hardness and found paper; a borrowed charcoal stick. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 6 because every later unit needs the year's studio routine, the practice log and the looking routine (the safe-studio, practice-log and look-describe-interpret models are retrieved here, where first needed). Value is the drawing technique every later drawing builds on, and the year's first critique (describe, ask, suggest) is held on the still lifes | Candidate: a work showing daily life in Oceania or Southeast Asia beside Lawrence and the kente, for range | Lawrence's series tells of Black Americans leaving the South for work and safety under segregation and violence: the unit picks panels on travel and work, names the reasons plainly in the series' own captions, and the sensitivity agent reads The Phillips Collection's pages and Lawrence's captions first. Kente: the agent reads a Ghanaian or museum source on the cloth's names and uses first; students study it by looking and do not copy a named pattern as costume. Posters: the unit picks posters on parks, reading, safety at work and travel, none on disease. Hazards and controls: charcoal and chalk pastel dust (no blowing, wiped with a damp cloth, pencil for a student with asthma); ink washable and non-toxic; rinse water carried in lidded cups; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | 6 | An inquiry of my own. *How do artists turn a question they care about into a work, and how can we tell whether a work says what we meant?* | 16 | va-g6-ideas; va-g6-revise | VA:Cr1.1.6a; VA:Cr1.2.6a; VA:Cr3.1.6a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Romare Bearden, The Block (United States, 1971, the Metropolitan Museum of Art), a collage of one Harlem block, described and linked; a large collage of paper gathered from city streets by Mark Bradford (United States, born 1961; the unit plan picks the work and records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to a museum's page; Henri Matisse, The Snail (France, 1953, Tate, London), painted paper cut and arranged, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: merging ideas and choosing a research method (va-g5-ideas) and the artist statement (va-g5-revise). VA:Cr1.1.6a: in groups of three, combine concepts to produce fresh ideas (an idea swap: each writes two concepts, the group joins them in pairs), with the joined ideas credited to the group on idea pages. VA:Cr1.2.6a: plan an inquiry into content that matters to the student: a question, two sources (looking, a short interview, a reading) and a first sketch, on a planning page; a public or invented topic is offered beside a personal one and marked the same. VA:Cr3.1.6a: check whether the work gets across what was meant: a partner retells what the collage says before the maker explains, the maker compares and changes the work to match, then writes the statement. Technique: collage and mixed media (tearing against cutting, layering, glue control) with papers painted in a color scheme (complementary or analogous). Words: inquiry, concept, source, intent, viewer, revise. Showing: the collages with their statements, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr1.1.6a, a guide look-for on the kept idea pages: at least two fresh ideas made by joining group members' concepts, with the joining shown; VA:Cr1.2.6a, a guide look-for on the kept planning page: a question, two sources and a first sketch that follows from them; VA:Cr3.1.6a, a written item in the check-day sitting on the student's own revised collage: what was meant, what the viewer saw, the change made and why, checked against the record sheet. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: strategies for being stuck (grade 7). Tools: the planning page, the retelling card and color-scheme step cards, Curio Class's own; a color-scheme practice tool or its printed wheel. Free path: old calendars, newspaper and scrap paper, glue from flour paste made at home if wanted. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 1 because collage needs value contrast and the habit of trying new materials; before unit 3 because a design starts from a planning page and the intent check. The year's main product, so it is long; the sketchbook, talk-about-choices and artist-statement models are retrieved here | Candidate: a collage or assemblage maker from Latin America or Asia beside the three | Content that matters to a student is invited, never required: the public or invented topic is offered beside it and marked the same. A question about a worry is handled with health's steps if a student tells something. Bearden's Harlem and Bradford's city papers: the sensitivity agent reads the museums' pages first. Hazards and controls: scissors; glue sticks and white glue, no spray adhesive; paint for papers premixed and water-based; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | 6 | Designed for someone. *How do designers meet the needs of the people who will use a thing, and how should we judge a design or an artwork?* | 14 | va-g6-design; va-g6-judge | VA:Cr2.3.6a; VA:Cn10.1.6a; VA:Re8.1.6a; VA:Re9.1.6a | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Inuit snow goggles (the Arctic, carved wood or bone with narrow slits to cut snow glare; the Smithsonian's National Museum of the American Indian, the unit plan picks the object and its date), described and linked; the sidewalk curb cut, won by disability rights activists in Berkeley, California, in the early 1970s, told from a public agency's or museum's page; Amrita Sher-Gil, Three Girls (India, 1935, the National Gallery of Modern Art, New Delhi), described and linked, judged with set criteria | Retrieval in day 2's opener: labeled sketches of places and objects (va-g5-design) and judging across cultures with a standards set (va-g5-judge). VA:Cn10.1.6a: gather a collection of ideas from current interests and worries that art-making could explore (the class idea wall, at least four of the student's own, public or invented ones marked the same), from which each student picks a design problem. VA:Cr2.3.6a: create or rework an object, place or system for the stated needs of varied users: interview two users (a classmate, a family member or an invented user card), write each user's needs on the design brief, build a cardboard model and note how each need is met. VA:Re8.1.6a: on Sher-Gil's painting, sort context cards into useful and unhelpful, then study subject, form, structure and media for its ideas and mood. VA:Re9.1.6a: create criteria for judging a work and use them, on Sher-Gil and on a classmate's model, with the evidence sentence (the new model: the judgment and the part of the work that shows it). Technique: cardboard construction: scoring with a blunt tool, folding, tab and slot, joining with tape and glue (any heavy cut by an adult with a blade). No scale drawing is asked (grade 7). Words: user, need, criteria, context, evidence, mood. Showing: the models with design notes at a user fair, for the users interviewed or a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cn10.1.6a, a kept card: the student's collection of at least four ideas from current interests and concerns, each marked with how art could explore it; VA:Cr2.3.6a, a guide look-for on the model and design note: each named user's stated need and how the model meets it; VA:Re8.1.6a, a written item: an unseen work with six context cards, the useful ones kept and one unhelpful one set aside with a reason, then the work's idea and mood from subject, form and media; VA:Re9.1.6a, a written item: criteria the student writes for the unseen work and a judgment against them in evidence sentences. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: information organized for a viewer (grade 7). Tools: the user interview card, the design brief and note, context cards and the criteria sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal boxes, tape and glue. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 2 because a design starts from a planning page and a user's retelling is an intent check; the evidence sentence starts here, before unit 4's source-based response. Its written judgment leans on ELA grade 6 unit 2's argument writing (see Cross-subject) | Candidate: a design for varied users from Africa or Asia, from an open-access collection, beside the goggles | Users: no student is asked about a classmate's disability or health; user cards include invented users with stated needs. The curb cut is told through the activists' own published words (the sensitivity agent reads a disability rights history source and the agency's page first). Inuit snow goggles: the agent reads the museum's page and an Inuit organization's or Inuit maker's published words first; the student's model is their own design, never a copy of an Inuit object. Worries on the idea wall: public or invented ideas count the same; health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: cardboard cut by an adult only; scissors; tape and glue; scoring with a blunt tool. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | 6 | Museums and the first art. *How does a museum show a community's history and values, and how has art changed with its time, customs and materials?* | 14 | va-g6-show; va-g6-museum | VA:Pr4.1.6a; VA:Pr5.1.6a; VA:Pr6.1.6a; VA:Cn11.1.6a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11). Works: the cave paintings of Lascaux (France, about 17,000 years ago; the French ministry of culture's site), described; the painted pig of Leang Tedongnge, Sulawesi, Indonesia, dated to at least 45,500 years ago by a 2021 study in Science Advances (as of that study), described from the study; the Ah-Tah-Thi-Ki Museum of the Seminole Tribe of Florida (Big Cypress Reservation), its own pages, with Seminole patchwork clothing as a case of art changing with materials (hand-cranked sewing machines from about the start of the twentieth century; the unit plan checks the date on the museum's pages) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the curator's argued choices (va-g5-show) and evidence from a real exhibit (va-g5-museum). VA:Pr4.1.6a: analyze how keeping and showing flat, 3D and digital art are alike and different (light, handling, mounts, file care), on a three-column chart. VA:Pr5.1.6a: alone or in a group, draw a display plan for the hallway or a classroom wall that weighs the space, the viewers (eye level, path) and the layout, from the year's kept work. VA:Pr6.1.6a: judge and explain with evidence how a museum mirrors its community's history and values (the Seminole museum's pages, or a local museum's), in a source-based response with a work card for each work. VA:Cn11.1.6a: analyze how art mirrors changing eras, customs, materials and uses (cave pigments, patchwork on new machines, a digital work), using social studies grade 6's dating and sourcing of early humans (see Cross-subject). Technique: mounting on card, labels (title, maker, date, medium), and the display plan drawn as a wall elevation, not to scale. Words: preserve, display plan, layout, viewer, community, era. Showing: the year-end hallway show to families, hung from the plans. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.6a, a written item: an unseen flat work and an unseen 3D or digital work, two likenesses and two differences in how each is kept and shown; VA:Pr5.1.6a, a guide look-for on the kept display plan: space, viewers and layout each weighed with a reason; VA:Pr6.1.6a, a kept source-based response made on the last making days: a claim on how the museum mirrors its community's history and values, with two pieces of evidence on work cards; VA:Cn11.1.6a, a written item: two unseen works from different eras, how era, custom and materials show in each. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: venues compared (grade 7). Tools: the display plan sheet, label cards, work cards and readings, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts, painter's tape. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in grade 6 because the show is planned and hung from the year's kept work (units 1 to 3), and the cave-art study follows social studies grade 6's early humans unit. It is assessed by its own end-of-unit assessment and the showing; the year-end check in week 32 then samples units 1 to 3 | Candidate: the Seminole museum's pages checked open and free on the day the unit plan is written, and a local museum's pages named as the alternative | The Seminole Tribe of Florida: the sensitivity agent reads the tribe's museum pages first; the removal wars are told in the museum's own words; no student copies a patchwork design. Cave art: dates and claims only as the study and the site state them. A student's work is shown outside the class only with the family's yes and under the student's chosen name. Hazards and controls: mount card cut by an adult or with scissors; glue sticks; tape. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 7 | Seen, shown, shared. *How do where and how we see a work change its value, and what do we owe others when we share images?* | 18 | va-g7-look; va-g7-craft | VA:Re7.1.7a; VA:Re7.2.7a; VA:Cr2.1.7a; VA:Cr2.2.7a | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2). Works: cast brass plaques of the Kingdom of Benin (now Nigeria, sixteenth to seventeenth centuries), taken by a British force in 1897, shown in museums abroad, and some returned to Nigeria from 2022 (as of the unit plan's date; the Smithsonian's National Museum of African Art's pages); Norman Rockwell, Freedom of Speech (United States, 1943, the Norman Rockwell Museum, Stockbridge, Massachusetts), painted for a weekly magazine and then printed on posters to sell war bonds, described and linked; Fan Kuan, Travelers among Mountains and Streams (China, Northern Song, about 1000, the National Palace Museum, Taipei, from its open data images), ink built up in layers | Retrieval in day 2's opener: interpreting how people live and how images sway (va-g6-look) and the environmental cost of supplies (va-g6-craft). VA:Re7.1.7a: explain how the way a work is displayed, where it is and the viewer's experience change how it is seen and valued (a plaque on a palace pillar, in a museum case abroad, back in Nigeria). VA:Re7.2.7a: analyze several ways one image sways particular audiences (Rockwell's painting for magazine readers and as a war bond poster). VA:Cr2.1.7a: keep at it, building skill in two media over at least four sessions on one piece, with a process log of what was tried and fixed each session. VA:Cr2.2.7a: show awareness of duty to self and others when sharing images online: a written sharing plan for a photo of the finished piece (the maker's permission, no faces or names, the caption, who may see it); nothing is posted. Technique: ink line over layered watercolor washes: flat wash, graded wash, wet into wet, dry brush, ink line over a dry wash, layered across sessions as Fan Kuan's ink is. Words: display, location, audience, persist, layer, permission. Showing: the finished pieces with their process logs, in the classroom. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Re7.1.7a, a written item: an unseen work shown in two settings (a museum case and a public square, on two cards), how display, place and experience change how it is seen and valued; VA:Re7.2.7a, a written item: an unseen public domain poster and two named audiences, one way it sways each; VA:Cr2.1.7a, a guide look-for on the kept piece and process log: worked across at least four sessions in two media, with a fix logged each session; VA:Cr2.2.7a, a kept card: the sharing plan, with one duty to self and one to others explained. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: copyright and open licenses (grade 8). Tools: wash step cards, the process log, the sharing plan card, work cards and readings, Curio Class's own; a work viewer or printed cards. Free path: a shared watercolor set per table and a black pen. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 7 because it reopens the studio, and its long piece needs the most sessions of the year; persistence over sessions comes before unit 6's strategies for being stuck. Display and audience open the year's thread on venues, which unit 8 finishes | Candidate: a work by a living maker shown both online and in a gallery, described and linked | The Benin plaques: the sensitivity agent reads the Smithsonian's pages on the 2022 return and a Nigerian museum body's published words first; the 1897 taking is told plainly as the museums tell it; objects made for royal ancestral altars are studied by looking only and are not imitated. Rockwell's wartime poster: the unit studies persuasion, not a war's rights and wrongs. Online sharing: no student posts anything, makes an account or photographs a face. Hazards and controls: water spills wiped at once; ink water-based and non-toxic; brushes washed; rinse water conserved as in unit 1 (review). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 7 | Getting unstuck. *What do artists do when they get stuck, and how can written guidelines steer a work to its aim?* | 14 | va-g7-ideas; va-g7-revise | VA:Cr1.1.7a; VA:Cr1.2.7a; VA:Cr3.1.7a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Gustave Caillebotte, Paris Street; Rainy Day (France, 1877, the Art Institute of Chicago, open access), a street in one-point perspective; Sol LeWitt's wall drawings, made by others from his written instructions (United States, from 1968; the unit plan picks one and links the holding museum's page), described and linked; a layered drawing built on architectural lines by Julie Mehretu (born 1970 in Ethiopia, works in the United States; the unit plan picks the work and records whether living, as of its date), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the planned inquiry (va-g6-ideas) and intent against a viewer's retelling (va-g6-revise). VA:Cr1.1.7a: use strategies to get past being stuck (change the material, set a limit, start from a different edge, work small and fast, borrow one rule from a studied work), each tried at a marked stuck point and logged. VA:Cr1.2.7a: write guidelines that steer a work toward a stated aim, as LeWitt's instructions do, then a partner tries them and says where they were unclear. VA:Cr3.1.7a: reflect on the finished perspective work and explain its key points (the aim, the choices, the change) in a format the student picks: a written statement, a labeled sketch, or a talk recorded as sound only on the school's device. Technique: one-point perspective: horizon line, vanishing point, lines to the point, a box, then a street or room, then the student's own perspective work. Words: stuck, strategy, guideline, aim, vanishing point, reflect. Showing: perspective works with their guidelines, on the classroom wall. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr1.1.7a, a guide look-for on the sketchbook: a stuck point marked and at least two strategies tried, with what each did; VA:Cr1.2.7a, a kept card: the written guidelines with the stated aim, and the partner's trial notes; VA:Cr3.1.7a, a written item in the check-day sitting (or a labeled sketch, or a sound-only talk recorded that day, the student's choice): the work's aim, two choices and one change explained. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: criteria for unfinished work (grade 8). Tools: perspective step cards, the guidelines card and the reflection choice sheet, Curio Class's own; a perspective guide tool or its printed grid. Free path: pencil, ruler or a card edge, scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 5 because a student gets stuck inside a long piece, and the strategies build on the process log; perspective builds on grade 6's value. Before unit 7 because written guidelines feed organizing a layout for a viewer. The talk-about-choices, critique-protocol and artist-statement models are retrieved here | Candidate: a perspective work from Asia or the Islamic world (a Persian or Mughal painting with its own space system) beside Caillebotte | A recorded talk is sound only, stays on the school's device and is never required. Hazards and controls: rulers and pencils; no blades (points sharpened by the teacher or with a hand sharpener). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 7 | 7 | Maps, layouts and gatherings. *How do artists and designers organize a work so viewers read it clearly, and where does our community gather for art?* | 14 | va-g7-design; va-g7-judge | VA:Cr2.3.7a; VA:Cn10.1.7a; VA:Re8.1.7a; VA:Re9.1.7a | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Charles Joseph Minard's 1869 map of the French army's march to Moscow and back (France, public domain; a library's digitized copy), information drawn so it reads at a glance; Jaune Quick-to-See Smith, State Names (2000, the Smithsonian American Art Museum), a map of the United States by a citizen of the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Nation (born 1940; the unit plan records her dates as of its date), described and linked; Piet Mondrian, Broadway Boogie Woogie (made in New York, 1942 to 1943, the Museum of Modern Art), described and linked, judged twice | Retrieval in day 2's opener: designing for users' needs (va-g6-design) and criteria with an evidence sentence (va-g6-judge). VA:Cn10.1.7a: alone or together, make a visual record of where and when people gather to make or see art in the community (public calendars, library and park pages, sketches or photographs with no faces), at least four places and times. VA:Cr2.3.7a: apply visual organizing strategies (hierarchy, grouping, contrast and scale) to make a poster-map of those gatherings that clearly shares the information, drawn with a stated scale. VA:Re8.1.7a: find State Names' meaning by weighing how it was made, its form and structure, its setting, subject and media. VA:Re9.1.7a: judge Mondrian's painting twice, once by personal taste and once by established criteria, and explain how the two judgments differ. Technique: information design (hierarchy by size and weight, grouping, a grid, contrast) and hand lettering for titles and labels, with a map drawn to a scale (math grade 7's scale drawings, see Cross-subject). Words: hierarchy, grouping, contrast, scale, taste, established criteria. Showing: the poster-maps displayed where the school community will read them. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cn10.1.7a, a kept card: the visual record of at least four places and times, with how each was found; VA:Cr2.3.7a, a guide look-for on the poster-map: hierarchy, grouping, contrast and a stated scale used so a partner finds the three main facts without help; VA:Re8.1.7a, a written item: an unseen work's meaning from its making, form, setting, subject and media; VA:Re9.1.7a, a written item: two judgments of an unseen work, by taste and by established criteria, and how they differ. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: pictures and words for an audience (grade 8). Tools: layout step cards, the gathering record sheet, context cards and the two-judgments sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil, ruler, scrap paper, a borrowed marker set. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6 because written guidelines become a layout's brief, and after math grade 7's scale unit; the judging thread moves from criteria made (grade 6) to taste told apart from criteria. Hands information design to unit 11's pictures and words | Candidate: an information design from Africa or Asia (a historic map or chart, open access) beside Minard | State Names speaks to Native lands and the names left on the map: the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page and Smith's own published words first. Gatherings: public places and public pages only; a family event is optional, with a public or invented option marked the same; no student visits a site alone for the task, and no photograph shows a face. Hazards and controls: rulers; markers labeled non-toxic. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 8 | 7 | Venues and versions. *How has technology changed the way art is kept and seen, and how does knowing a work's time, place and use change our reaction?* | 14 | va-g7-show; va-g7-museum | VA:Pr4.1.7a; VA:Pr5.1.7a; VA:Pr6.1.7a; VA:Cn11.1.7a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11). Works: the terracotta army of the first Qin emperor (China, about 210 BCE; the emperor's mausoleum site museum, Xi'an), seen in its pits, in traveling exhibitions and on screens; a Plains ledger drawing (late nineteenth century, drawn on account-book paper; the unit plan picks one from an open-access collection with its maker and nation, such as a drawing made at Fort Marion, Florida, between 1875 and 1878) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the drawn display plan (va-g6-show) and a museum judged with evidence (va-g6-museum). VA:Pr4.1.7a: compare how technology has changed the way art is kept, shown and seen (photography, printing, climate control, screens), with the terracotta army as the case. VA:Pr5.1.7a: judge two ways of readying the class's work for showing (mounted on the wall, a printed catalog page, a slideshow or its paper storyboard) against a set checklist. VA:Pr6.1.7a: compare what it is like to view a collection in different venues (the pits at the site, a traveling show, a screen; the class's wall against its catalog). VA:Cn11.1.7a: analyze how knowing a work's era, place, materials and use shapes a reaction: a first reaction to a ledger drawing, then again with its setting card. Technique: preparing work three ways (mounting, a catalog page with image and label, a slideshow storyboard). Words: venue, technology, preserve, checklist, setting, reaction. Showing: the year-end class show in two venues, the room wall and the printed catalog (or a slideshow on the teacher screen), for families. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.7a, a written item: one technology and how it changed the keeping, showing or seeing of an unseen work; VA:Pr5.1.7a, a kept card: the checklist scores for two methods, with a reason for each score; VA:Pr6.1.7a, a written item: an unseen collection in two venues, compared; VA:Cn11.1.7a, a kept card made on the last making day: the student's first reaction to the ledger drawing, the reaction after the setting card, and what in the setting changed it. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a show juried by criteria (grade 8). Tools: the methods checklist, catalog page template, setting cards and readings, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts, one printed catalog page per table. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in grade 7 because the show is prepared from the year's kept work and finishes the venues thread unit 5 opened; assessed by its own end-of-unit assessment and the showing, before the year-end check in week 32 | Candidate: a born-digital work, shown only on screens, described and linked, as a third venue | Ledger drawings: made by Plains men held as prisoners at Fort Marion; the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and a source from the maker's nation first; the drawing is studied by looking and never imitated. The terracotta army was made for a burial: studied by looking only. Family yes and chosen name for the show and the catalog. Hazards and controls: mount card cut by an adult or with scissors; glue sticks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 9 | 8 | Taste, image and borrowing. *How do culture and surroundings shape the image a person shows the world, and when may an artist borrow someone else's image?* | 18 | va-g8-look; va-g8-craft | VA:Re7.1.8a; VA:Re7.2.8a; VA:Cr2.1.8a; VA:Cr2.2.8a | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2). Works: Kehinde Wiley, President Barack Obama (United States, 2018, the Smithsonian's National Portrait Gallery; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; Hyacinthe Rigaud, Louis XIV (France, 1701, the Louvre, public domain), the image a king chose to show; a painting in the style of a hand-painted shop sign, with words, by Cheri Samba (Democratic Republic of the Congo, born 1956; the unit plan picks the work and records whether living), described and linked; for copyright, the United States Supreme Court's 2023 fair-use decision on a silkscreen portrait made from a photographer's picture (Andy Warhol Foundation for the Visual Arts v. Goldsmith, 598 U.S. 508), told from the Court's own opinion | Retrieval in day 2's opener: display, place and audience (va-g7-look) and the process log and duty when sharing (va-g7-craft). VA:Re7.1.8a: explain how culture and surroundings shape a person's taste and the image they project, from the two portraits (what each sitter chose to show, and what their times valued) and invented personas, never the student's own looks. VA:Re7.2.8a: compare the settings and media where viewers meet images that sway them (one image on a billboard, a phone feed and a gallery wall, on printed cards). VA:Cr2.1.8a: take a planned risk in the print (a new scale, subject or way of working) and log what it meant to the work. VA:Cr2.2.8a: explain how copyright, fair use, borrowing and open licenses apply to the student's own work, labeling every borrowed image in the print as the student's own, open license or public domain, with credit. Technique: relief printmaking on a foam plate: drawing into the plate with a blunt pencil, inking with a brayer, two-color printing with registration, an edition of three. Words: taste, project, setting, risk, copyright, open license. Showing: the print editions with source labels, in a print rack or on a line. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Re7.1.8a, a written item: an unseen public domain portrait, how its culture and surroundings shaped the image it projects; VA:Re7.2.8a, a written item: one unseen image in two settings or media, compared for how it sways; VA:Cr2.1.8a, a guide look-for on the edition and the risk log: a risk planned, taken and what it meant; VA:Cr2.2.8a, a kept card: a source label for every borrowed image, with which rule applies to each (copyright, fair use, open license, public domain) and why. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: health effects of materials and hazard sheets (Level 1). Tools: print step cards, the risk log, source-label cards, setting cards and the case reading, Curio Class's own; a work viewer or printed cards. Free path: clean foam trays bought new for the purpose, tempera thickened with a little dish soap or glue for ink, a spoon to rub the print by hand. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 8 because a two-color edition is the band's hardest making technique and needs the most sessions; copyright labels come before units 10 and 11, where groups borrow images for their inquiry and zine | Candidate: a self-portrait by a living maker from Asia or Latin America beside the two portraits, described and linked | Portraits: no task asks about a student's own appearance, body, clothes or culture; the image a person projects is studied through the portraits and invented personas (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). The Obama portrait is studied as a painting; no political view is asked or marked. Samba's paintings often mock public life: the unit plan picks one with no adult content, and the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page first. Hazards and controls: water-based block ink labeled non-toxic only, no solvents; brayers rolled on a covered table; no blades; foam trays new or washed, never from raw meat; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 10 | 8 | Art of everyday life now. *How do artists today look into everyday life, and how do we judge a work still in progress and plan its changes?* | 14 | va-g8-ideas; va-g8-revise | VA:Cr1.1.8a; VA:Cr1.2.8a; VA:Cr3.1.8a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Leonardo da Vinci's notebook pages (Italy, about 1478 to 1518, the Codex Arundel, the British Library, digitized, public domain), a record of early steps in pictures and words; fabric versions of the homes he lived in by Do Ho Suh (born 1962 in Korea; the unit plan picks the work and records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to a museum's page; the class's own records | Retrieval in day 2's opener: strategies when stuck and written guidelines (va-g7-ideas) and a reflection in a chosen format (va-g7-revise). VA:Cr1.1.8a: record early creative steps in pictures or words, as they happen, in an old or new medium (sketchbook pages, a photo log of the pages on the school's device). VA:Cr1.2.8a: as a group, shape an inquiry into one part of present-day life using a current practice of art or design (for example, how people use a shared space at school, studied by observing, gesture drawing and mapping as artists who make work from daily life do), with each student's part on the plan. VA:Cr3.1.8a: apply fitting criteria to examine and think over the unfinished work, then plan changes in writing before making them. Technique: gesture and figure drawing: one-minute to five-minute gesture studies, then longer figure studies, with the figure about seven and a half heads tall as a rough guide (counted, no ratio), drawn from a jointed figure, from public pictures or from classmates who choose to pose, clothed. Words: record, process, inquiry, present-day, gesture, criteria. Showing: each group's inquiry works and records, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr1.1.8a, a guide look-for on the kept record: early steps recorded as they happened over at least three sessions, in pictures or words; VA:Cr1.2.8a, a kept card: the group's inquiry plan (the part of present-day life, the current practice used, the student's own part), one card a student; VA:Cr3.1.8a, a written item: a Curio Class original shown unfinished, criteria chosen and applied and two changes planned in writing (the student's own written revision plan from the critique day is carried unscored). Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: standards from other cultures for review (Level 1). Tools: gesture step cards, the record page, the group plan card and the revision plan sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil, charcoal or a marker, newsprint or scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 9 because the risk log becomes the record of early steps, and before unit 11 because the group inquiry is the class's first group work of the year and readies the group mural. Criteria for unfinished work build on grade 7's guidelines | Candidate: a living maker from Africa or Latin America whose practice studies everyday life, described and linked | Figure drawing: clothed only, posing is a choice, and no comment is made on anyone's body; observation at school records no faces and no names, and no photograph of a person is taken without the family's yes. Hazards and controls: charcoal dust as in unit 1 (review); no blades. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 11 | 8 | Words, pictures and us. *How do pictures and words work together to carry a message, and how can art made together strengthen a group?* | 16 | va-g8-design; va-g8-judge | VA:Cr2.3.8a; VA:Cn10.1.8a; VA:Re8.1.8a; VA:Re9.1.8a | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Judith F. Baca, The Great Wall of Los Angeles (United States, begun 1974, in the Tujunga Wash flood channel, painted with hundreds of young people; the unit plan records whether Baca is living, as of its date), described and linked to the mural's own pages; screen prints of words and pictures by Corita Kent (United States, 1918 to 1986; the Corita Art Center's pages), described and linked; Wifredo Lam, The Jungle (Cuba, 1943, the Museum of Modern Art), described and linked, for the argued judgment | Retrieval in day 2's opener: visual organizing strategies (va-g7-design) and taste against established criteria (va-g7-judge). VA:Cr2.3.8a: pick, arrange and design pictures and text for a clear, compelling presentation: a zine spread or a three-poster series on the group inquiry of unit 10, with lettering and images on a grid. VA:Cn10.1.8a: with a group, make a paper-panel mural that reflects on and strengthens the good side of a group the class shares (the class, the school, a team or club, or an invented group); no student is asked to stand for their own ethnicity, faith or family. VA:Re8.1.8a: write an analysis of how subject, form, structure, media, approach and setting together build a work's message and mood (Lam's painting, then Baca's panels). VA:Re9.1.8a: argue for a judgment of a work with solid reasons: a claim, reasons, evidence from the work and an answer to an opposing view (ELA grade 8 unit 2's argument writing, see Cross-subject). Technique: pictures and words on a grid (hand lettering, type size and weight, image and text hierarchy) for the spread; panels joined by a shared grid and color plan for the mural. Words: layout, message, audience, group identity, claim, counterclaim. Showing: the mural and the zines, shown to the school. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr2.3.8a, a guide look-for on the zine spread or posters: pictures and text chosen and arranged so the message reads first and the audience is named; VA:Cn10.1.8a, a guide look-for on the student's mural panel and the group's note: the panel's part in the group's chosen positive theme, said in the note; VA:Re8.1.8a, a written item (first check day): an unseen work's message and mood from how its subject, form, media, approach and setting work together; VA:Re9.1.8a, a written item (second check day): an argued judgment of an unseen work, with a claim, two reasons, evidence and a counterclaim answered. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a team proposal for a real space (Level 1). Tools: layout step cards, the mural grid plan, the group note card and the argument frame, Curio Class's own. Free path: scrap paper panels taped together, a borrowed marker set. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 10 because the zine presents the group inquiry and the mural needs a group that has worked together; after ELA grade 8's argument unit. Hands pictures and words to unit 12's exhibition stories | Candidate: a group or community art project from Africa or Asia beside Baca's mural, described and linked | Group identity: the group is one the class shares or an invented one, never a student's ethnicity, faith or family, and the task strengthens what is good in it. Baca's mural tells California's history, including hard history (the forced removal of Japanese Americans, segregation); the unit picks panels, and the sensitivity agent reads the mural's own pages and Baca's published words first. Lam's painting draws on Afro-Cuban religious figures: the agent reads the museum's page first; it is studied by looking and never imitated. Corita Kent was a member of a religious order: said in one plain sentence. Hazards and controls: markers labeled non-toxic, no aerosol paint; long paper on tables or the floor, with a clear walkway. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 12 | 8 | The show and the group. *How can a show change what visitors think, and how does art stand for and build a group's identity?* | 12 | va-g8-show; va-g8-museum | VA:Pr4.1.8a; VA:Pr5.1.8a; VA:Pr6.1.8a; VA:Cn11.1.8a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11). Works: Nation to Nation: Treaties Between the United States and American Indian Nations, an exhibition at the Smithsonian's National Museum of the American Indian, Washington (opened 2014; its pages, as of the unit plan's date); a pole carved by Robert Davidson (Haida, born 1946; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date) and raised in Old Massett, Haida Gwaii, in 1969, the first pole raised there in many decades (the unit plan checks the account on the Haida Gwaii Museum's or the artist's own pages) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: methods scored against a checklist (va-g7-show) and setting shaping a reaction (va-g7-museum). VA:Pr4.1.8a: create criteria and use them to judge a group of works for a show (the class juries portfolio pieces into a theme). VA:Pr5.1.8a: with a group, ready and hang the themed show and write its exhibition stories (an introduction panel and section texts) for viewers. VA:Pr6.1.8a: examine why and how a show or collection can influence visitors' thoughts, beliefs and experiences (the treaty exhibition's choices; the visitors' notes left at the class show). VA:Cn11.1.8a: tell apart the ways art stands for, builds, strengthens and mirrors a group's identity (Davidson's pole, Baca's mural from unit 11, a team's emblem), in a sort. Technique: jurying with criteria, sequencing works, sight lines and spacing, and writing wall stories to a word limit. Words: jury, criteria, theme, exhibition story, influence, identity. Showing: the year-end themed show for families and the school. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.8a, a kept card: the student's criteria and the jury sheet applied to the group of works; VA:Pr5.1.8a, a guide look-for on the showing: the student's part of the hang and the exhibition story they wrote; VA:Pr6.1.8a, a written item: an unseen exhibition described on a Curio Class card, how its choices may influence a visitor's thoughts or beliefs; VA:Cn11.1.8a, a written item: four unseen works sorted by the way each serves a group's identity (stands for, builds, strengthens, mirrors), with a reason for two. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an exhibit's effect on society and politics (Level 1). Tools: the jury sheet, the story-writing card, the identity sort and readings, Curio Class's own. Free path: painter's tape, cereal-box mounts, hand-written labels. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in the band because the show is juried from the year's kept work and hands the curating and museum threads to Level 1; assessed by its own end-of-unit assessment and the showing, before the year-end check in week 32 | Candidate: a community museum or show from Africa, Asia or Oceania that tells a group's own story, beside the treaty exhibition | The Haida pole: crest figures belong to lineages; the pole is studied by looking only, and no student carves or draws a Haida crest; the sensitivity agent reads the Haida Gwaii Museum's and the artist's own published words first. The treaty exhibition: treaties made and broken are told in the museum's own words, read first by the agent. A student's work is shown outside the class only with the family's yes and under the student's chosen name. Hazards and controls: work hung with tape or clips by students on the floor, any hanging above shoulder height by an adult; no ladders for students. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

