264: | 3 | 6 | Designed for someone. *How do designers meet the needs of the people who will use a thing, and how should we judge a design or an artwork?* | 14 | va-g6-design; va-g6-judge | VA:Cr2.3.6a; VA:Cn10.1.6a; VA:Re8.1.6a; VA:Re9.1.6a | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Inuit snow goggles (the Arctic, carved wood or bone with narrow slits to cut snow glare; the Smithsonian's National Museum of the American Indian, the unit plan picks the object and its date), described and linked; the sidewalk curb cut, won by disability rights activists in Berkeley, California, in the early 1970s, told from a public agency's or museum's page; Amrita Sher-Gil, Three Girls (India, 1935, the National Gallery of Modern Art, New Delhi), described and linked, judged with set criteria | Retrieval in day 2's opener: labeled sketches of places and objects (va-g5-design) and judging across cultures with a standards set (va-g5-judge). VA:Cn10.1.6a: gather a collection of ideas from current interests and worries that art-making could explore (the class idea wall, at least four of the student's own, public or invented ones marked the same), from which each student picks a design problem. VA:Cr2.3.6a: create or rework an object, place or system for the stated needs of varied users: interview two users (a classmate, a family member or an invented user card), write each user's needs on the design brief, build a cardboard model and note how each need is met. VA:Re8.1.6a: on Sher-Gil's painting, sort context cards into useful and unhelpful, then study subject, form, structure and media for its ideas and mood. VA:Re9.1.6a: create criteria for judging a work and use them, on Sher-Gil and on a classmate's model, with the evidence sentence (the new model: the judgment and the part of the work that shows it). Technique: cardboard construction: scoring with a blunt tool, folding, tab and slot, joining with tape and glue (any heavy cut by an adult with a blade). No scale drawing is asked (grade 7). Words: user, need, criteria, context, evidence, mood. Showing: the models with design notes at a user fair, for the users interviewed or a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cn10.1.6a, a kept card: the student's collection of at least four ideas from current interests and concerns, each marked with how art could explore it; VA:Cr2.3.6a, a guide look-for on the model and design note: each named user's stated need and how the model meets it; VA:Re8.1.6a, a written item: an unseen work with six context cards, the useful ones kept and one unhelpful one set aside with a reason, then the work's idea and mood from subject, form and media; VA:Re9.1.6a, a written item: criteria the student writes for the unseen work and a judgment against them in evidence sentences. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: information organized for a viewer (grade 7). Tools: the user interview card, the design brief and note, context cards and the criteria sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal boxes, tape and glue. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 2 because a design starts from a planning page and a user's retelling is an intent check; the evidence sentence starts here, before unit 4's source-based response. Its written judgment leans on ELA grade 6 unit 2's argument writing (see Cross-subject) | Candidate: a design for varied users from Africa or Asia, from an open-access collection, beside the goggles | Users: no student is asked about a classmate's disability or health; user cards include invented users with stated needs. The curb cut is told through the activists' own published words (the sensitivity agent reads a disability rights history source and the agency's page first). Inuit snow goggles: the agent reads the museum's page and an Inuit organization's or Inuit maker's published words first; the student's model is their own design, never a copy of an Inuit object. Worries on the idea wall: public or invented ideas count the same; health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: cardboard cut by an adult only; scissors; tape and glue; scoring with a blunt tool. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
265: | 4 | 6 | Museums and the first art. *How does a museum show a community's history and values, and how has art changed with its time, customs and materials?* | 14 | va-g6-show; va-g6-museum | VA:Pr4.1.6a; VA:Pr5.1.6a; VA:Pr6.1.6a; VA:Cn11.1.6a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11). Works: the cave paintings of Lascaux (France, about 17,000 years ago; the French ministry of culture's site), described; the painted pig of Leang Tedongnge, Sulawesi, Indonesia, dated to at least 45,500 years ago by a 2021 study in Science Advances (as of that study), described from the study; the Ah-Tah-Thi-Ki Museum of the Seminole Tribe of Florida (Big Cypress Reservation), its own pages, with Seminole patchwork clothing as a case of art changing with materials (hand-cranked sewing machines from about the start of the twentieth century; the unit plan checks the date on the museum's pages) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the curator's argued choices (va-g5-show) and evidence from a real exhibit (va-g5-museum). VA:Pr4.1.6a: analyze how keeping and showing flat, 3D and digital art are alike and different (light, handling, mounts, file care), on a three-column chart. VA:Pr5.1.6a: alone or in a group, draw a display plan for the hallway or a classroom wall that weighs the space, the viewers (eye level, path) and the layout, from the year's kept work. VA:Pr6.1.6a: judge and explain with evidence how a museum mirrors its community's history and values (the Seminole museum's pages, or a local museum's), in a source-based response with a work card for each work. VA:Cn11.1.6a: analyze how art mirrors changing eras, customs, materials and uses (cave pigments, patchwork on new machines, a digital work), using social studies grade 6's dating and sourcing of early humans (see Cross-subject). Technique: mounting on card, labels (title, maker, date, medium), and the display plan drawn as a wall elevation, not to scale. Words: preserve, display plan, layout, viewer, community, era. Showing: the year-end hallway show to families, hung from the plans. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.6a, a written item: an unseen flat work and an unseen 3D or digital work, two likenesses and two differences in how each is kept and shown; VA:Pr5.1.6a, a guide look-for on the kept display plan: space, viewers and layout each weighed with a reason; VA:Pr6.1.6a, a kept source-based response made on the last making days: a claim on how the museum mirrors its community's history and values, with two pieces of evidence on work cards; VA:Cn11.1.6a, a written item: two unseen works from different eras, how era, custom and materials show in each. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: venues compared (grade 7). Tools: the display plan sheet, label cards, work cards and readings, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts, painter's tape. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in grade 6 because the show is planned and hung from the year's kept work (units 1 to 3), and the cave-art study follows social studies grade 6's early humans unit. It is assessed by its own end-of-unit assessment and the showing; the year-end check in week 32 then samples units 1 to 3 | Candidate: the Seminole museum's pages checked open and free on the day the unit plan is written, and a local museum's pages named as the alternative | The Seminole Tribe of Florida: the sensitivity agent reads the tribe's museum pages first; the removal wars are told in the museum's own words; no student copies a patchwork design. Cave art: dates and claims only as the study and the site state them. A student's work is shown outside the class only with the family's yes and under the student's chosen name. Hazards and controls: mount card cut by an adult or with scissors; glue sticks; tape. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
266: | 5 | 7 | Seen, shown, shared. *How do where and how we see a work change its value, and what do we owe others when we share images?* | 18 | va-g7-look; va-g7-craft | VA:Re7.1.7a; VA:Re7.2.7a; VA:Cr2.1.7a; VA:Cr2.2.7a | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2). Works: cast brass plaques of the Kingdom of Benin (now Nigeria, sixteenth to seventeenth centuries), taken by a British force in 1897, shown in museums abroad, and some returned to Nigeria from 2022 (as of the unit plan's date; the Smithsonian's National Museum of African Art's pages); Norman Rockwell, Freedom of Speech (United States, 1943, the Norman Rockwell Museum, Stockbridge, Massachusetts), painted for a weekly magazine and then printed on posters to sell war bonds, described and linked; Fan Kuan, Travelers among Mountains and Streams (China, Northern Song, about 1000, the National Palace Museum, Taipei, from its open data images), ink built up in layers | Retrieval in day 2's opener: interpreting how people live and how images sway (va-g6-look) and the environmental cost of supplies (va-g6-craft). VA:Re7.1.7a: explain how the way a work is displayed, where it is and the viewer's experience change how it is seen and valued (a plaque on a palace pillar, in a museum case abroad, back in Nigeria). VA:Re7.2.7a: analyze several ways one image sways particular audiences (Rockwell's painting for magazine readers and as a war bond poster). VA:Cr2.1.7a: keep at it, building skill in two media over at least four sessions on one piece, with a process log of what was tried and fixed each session. VA:Cr2.2.7a: show awareness of duty to self and others when sharing images online: a written sharing plan for a photo of the finished piece (the maker's permission, no faces or names, the caption, who may see it); nothing is posted. Technique: ink line over layered watercolor washes: flat wash, graded wash, wet into wet, dry brush, ink line over a dry wash, layered across sessions as Fan Kuan's ink is. Words: display, location, audience, persist, layer, permission. Showing: the finished pieces with their process logs, in the classroom. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Re7.1.7a, a written item: an unseen work shown in two settings (a museum case and a public square, on two cards), how display, place and experience change how it is seen and valued; VA:Re7.2.7a, a written item: an unseen public domain poster and two named audiences, one way it sways each; VA:Cr2.1.7a, a guide look-for on the kept piece and process log: worked across at least four sessions in two media, with a fix logged each session; VA:Cr2.2.7a, a kept card: the sharing plan, with one duty to self and one to others explained. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: copyright and open licenses (grade 8). Tools: wash step cards, the process log, the sharing plan card, work cards and readings, Curio Class's own; a work viewer or printed cards. Free path: a shared watercolor set per table and a black pen. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 7 because it reopens the studio, and its long piece needs the most sessions of the year; persistence over sessions comes before unit 6's strategies for being stuck. Display and audience open the year's thread on venues, which unit 8 finishes | Candidate: a work by a living maker shown both online and in a gallery, described and linked | The Benin plaques: the sensitivity agent reads the Smithsonian's pages on the 2022 return and a Nigerian museum body's published words first; the 1897 taking is told plainly as the museums tell it; objects made for royal ancestral altars are studied by looking only and are not imitated. Rockwell's wartime poster: the unit studies persuasion, not a war's rights and wrongs. Online sharing: no student posts anything, makes an account or photographs a face. Hazards and controls: water spills wiped at once; ink water-based and non-toxic; brushes washed; rinse water conserved as in unit 1 (review). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
267: | 6 | 7 | Getting unstuck. *What do artists do when they get stuck, and how can written guidelines steer a work to its aim?* | 14 | va-g7-ideas; va-g7-revise | VA:Cr1.1.7a; VA:Cr1.2.7a; VA:Cr3.1.7a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Gustave Caillebotte, Paris Street; Rainy Day (France, 1877, the Art Institute of Chicago, open access), a street in one-point perspective; Sol LeWitt's wall drawings, made by others from his written instructions (United States, from 1968; the unit plan picks one and links the holding museum's page), described and linked; a layered drawing built on architectural lines by Julie Mehretu (born 1970 in Ethiopia, works in the United States; the unit plan picks the work and records whether living, as of its date), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the planned inquiry (va-g6-ideas) and intent against a viewer's retelling (va-g6-revise). VA:Cr1.1.7a: use strategies to get past being stuck (change the material, set a limit, start from a different edge, work small and fast, borrow one rule from a studied work), each tried at a marked stuck point and logged. VA:Cr1.2.7a: write guidelines that steer a work toward a stated aim, as LeWitt's instructions do, then a partner tries them and says where they were unclear. VA:Cr3.1.7a: reflect on the finished perspective work and explain its key points (the aim, the choices, the change) in a format the student picks: a written statement, a labeled sketch, or a talk recorded as sound only on the school's device. Technique: one-point perspective: horizon line, vanishing point, lines to the point, a box, then a street or room, then the student's own perspective work. Words: stuck, strategy, guideline, aim, vanishing point, reflect. Showing: perspective works with their guidelines, on the classroom wall. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr1.1.7a, a guide look-for on the sketchbook: a stuck point marked and at least two strategies tried, with what each did; VA:Cr1.2.7a, a kept card: the written guidelines with the stated aim, and the partner's trial notes; VA:Cr3.1.7a, a written item in the check-day sitting (or a labeled sketch, or a sound-only talk recorded that day, the student's choice): the work's aim, two choices and one change explained. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: criteria for unfinished work (grade 8). Tools: perspective step cards, the guidelines card and the reflection choice sheet, Curio Class's own; a perspective guide tool or its printed grid. Free path: pencil, ruler or a card edge, scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 5 because a student gets stuck inside a long piece, and the strategies build on the process log; perspective builds on grade 6's value. Before unit 7 because written guidelines feed organizing a layout for a viewer. The talk-about-choices, critique-protocol and artist-statement models are retrieved here | Candidate: a perspective work from Asia or the Islamic world (a Persian or Mughal painting with its own space system) beside Caillebotte | A recorded talk is sound only, stays on the school's device and is never required. Hazards and controls: rulers and pencils; no blades (points sharpened by the teacher or with a hand sharpener). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

