268: | 7 | 7 | Maps, layouts and gatherings. *How do artists and designers organize a work so viewers read it clearly, and where does our community gather for art?* | 14 | va-g7-design; va-g7-judge | VA:Cr2.3.7a; VA:Cn10.1.7a; VA:Re8.1.7a; VA:Re9.1.7a | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Charles Joseph Minard's 1869 map of the French army's march to Moscow and back (France, public domain; a library's digitized copy), information drawn so it reads at a glance; Jaune Quick-to-See Smith, State Names (2000, the Smithsonian American Art Museum), a map of the United States by a citizen of the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Nation (born 1940; the unit plan records her dates as of its date), described and linked; Piet Mondrian, Broadway Boogie Woogie (made in New York, 1942 to 1943, the Museum of Modern Art), described and linked, judged twice | Retrieval in day 2's opener: designing for users' needs (va-g6-design) and criteria with an evidence sentence (va-g6-judge). VA:Cn10.1.7a: alone or together, make a visual record of where and when people gather to make or see art in the community (public calendars, library and park pages, sketches or photographs with no faces), at least four places and times. VA:Cr2.3.7a: apply visual organizing strategies (hierarchy, grouping, contrast and scale) to make a poster-map of those gatherings that clearly shares the information, drawn with a stated scale. VA:Re8.1.7a: find State Names' meaning by weighing how it was made, its form and structure, its setting, subject and media. VA:Re9.1.7a: judge Mondrian's painting twice, once by personal taste and once by established criteria, and explain how the two judgments differ. Technique: information design (hierarchy by size and weight, grouping, a grid, contrast) and hand lettering for titles and labels, with a map drawn to a scale (math grade 7's scale drawings, see Cross-subject). Words: hierarchy, grouping, contrast, scale, taste, established criteria. Showing: the poster-maps displayed where the school community will read them. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cn10.1.7a, a kept card: the visual record of at least four places and times, with how each was found; VA:Cr2.3.7a, a guide look-for on the poster-map: hierarchy, grouping, contrast and a stated scale used so a partner finds the three main facts without help; VA:Re8.1.7a, a written item: an unseen work's meaning from its making, form, setting, subject and media; VA:Re9.1.7a, a written item: two judgments of an unseen work, by taste and by established criteria, and how they differ. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: pictures and words for an audience (grade 8). Tools: layout step cards, the gathering record sheet, context cards and the two-judgments sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil, ruler, scrap paper, a borrowed marker set. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6 because written guidelines become a layout's brief, and after math grade 7's scale unit; the judging thread moves from criteria made (grade 6) to taste told apart from criteria. Hands information design to unit 11's pictures and words | Candidate: an information design from Africa or Asia (a historic map or chart, open access) beside Minard | State Names speaks to Native lands and the names left on the map: the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page and Smith's own published words first. Gatherings: public places and public pages only; a family event is optional, with a public or invented option marked the same; no student visits a site alone for the task, and no photograph shows a face. Hazards and controls: rulers; markers labeled non-toxic. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
269: | 8 | 7 | Venues and versions. *How has technology changed the way art is kept and seen, and how does knowing a work's time, place and use change our reaction?* | 14 | va-g7-show; va-g7-museum | VA:Pr4.1.7a; VA:Pr5.1.7a; VA:Pr6.1.7a; VA:Cn11.1.7a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11). Works: the terracotta army of the first Qin emperor (China, about 210 BCE; the emperor's mausoleum site museum, Xi'an), seen in its pits, in traveling exhibitions and on screens; a Plains ledger drawing (late nineteenth century, drawn on account-book paper; the unit plan picks one from an open-access collection with its maker and nation, such as a drawing made at Fort Marion, Florida, between 1875 and 1878) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the drawn display plan (va-g6-show) and a museum judged with evidence (va-g6-museum). VA:Pr4.1.7a: compare how technology has changed the way art is kept, shown and seen (photography, printing, climate control, screens), with the terracotta army as the case. VA:Pr5.1.7a: judge two ways of readying the class's work for showing (mounted on the wall, a printed catalog page, a slideshow or its paper storyboard) against a set checklist. VA:Pr6.1.7a: compare what it is like to view a collection in different venues (the pits at the site, a traveling show, a screen; the class's wall against its catalog). VA:Cn11.1.7a: analyze how knowing a work's era, place, materials and use shapes a reaction: a first reaction to a ledger drawing, then again with its setting card. Technique: preparing work three ways (mounting, a catalog page with image and label, a slideshow storyboard). Words: venue, technology, preserve, checklist, setting, reaction. Showing: the year-end class show in two venues, the room wall and the printed catalog (or a slideshow on the teacher screen), for families. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.7a, a written item: one technology and how it changed the keeping, showing or seeing of an unseen work; VA:Pr5.1.7a, a kept card: the checklist scores for two methods, with a reason for each score; VA:Pr6.1.7a, a written item: an unseen collection in two venues, compared; VA:Cn11.1.7a, a kept card made on the last making day: the student's first reaction to the ledger drawing, the reaction after the setting card, and what in the setting changed it. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a show juried by criteria (grade 8). Tools: the methods checklist, catalog page template, setting cards and readings, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts, one printed catalog page per table. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in grade 7 because the show is prepared from the year's kept work and finishes the venues thread unit 5 opened; assessed by its own end-of-unit assessment and the showing, before the year-end check in week 32 | Candidate: a born-digital work, shown only on screens, described and linked, as a third venue | Ledger drawings: made by Plains men held as prisoners at Fort Marion; the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and a source from the maker's nation first; the drawing is studied by looking and never imitated. The terracotta army was made for a burial: studied by looking only. Family yes and chosen name for the show and the catalog. Hazards and controls: mount card cut by an adult or with scissors; glue sticks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
270: | 9 | 8 | Taste, image and borrowing. *How do culture and surroundings shape the image a person shows the world, and when may an artist borrow someone else's image?* | 18 | va-g8-look; va-g8-craft | VA:Re7.1.8a; VA:Re7.2.8a; VA:Cr2.1.8a; VA:Cr2.2.8a | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2). Works: Kehinde Wiley, President Barack Obama (United States, 2018, the Smithsonian's National Portrait Gallery; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; Hyacinthe Rigaud, Louis XIV (France, 1701, the Louvre, public domain), the image a king chose to show; a painting in the style of a hand-painted shop sign, with words, by Cheri Samba (Democratic Republic of the Congo, born 1956; the unit plan picks the work and records whether living), described and linked; for copyright, the United States Supreme Court's 2023 fair-use decision on a silkscreen portrait made from a photographer's picture (Andy Warhol Foundation for the Visual Arts v. Goldsmith, 598 U.S. 508), told from the Court's own opinion | Retrieval in day 2's opener: display, place and audience (va-g7-look) and the process log and duty when sharing (va-g7-craft). VA:Re7.1.8a: explain how culture and surroundings shape a person's taste and the image they project, from the two portraits (what each sitter chose to show, and what their times valued) and invented personas, never the student's own looks. VA:Re7.2.8a: compare the settings and media where viewers meet images that sway them (one image on a billboard, a phone feed and a gallery wall, on printed cards). VA:Cr2.1.8a: take a planned risk in the print (a new scale, subject or way of working) and log what it meant to the work. VA:Cr2.2.8a: explain how copyright, fair use, borrowing and open licenses apply to the student's own work, labeling every borrowed image in the print as the student's own, open license or public domain, with credit. Technique: relief printmaking on a foam plate: drawing into the plate with a blunt pencil, inking with a brayer, two-color printing with registration, an edition of three. Words: taste, project, setting, risk, copyright, open license. Showing: the print editions with source labels, in a print rack or on a line. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Re7.1.8a, a written item: an unseen public domain portrait, how its culture and surroundings shaped the image it projects; VA:Re7.2.8a, a written item: one unseen image in two settings or media, compared for how it sways; VA:Cr2.1.8a, a guide look-for on the edition and the risk log: a risk planned, taken and what it meant; VA:Cr2.2.8a, a kept card: a source label for every borrowed image, with which rule applies to each (copyright, fair use, open license, public domain) and why. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: health effects of materials and hazard sheets (Level 1). Tools: print step cards, the risk log, source-label cards, setting cards and the case reading, Curio Class's own; a work viewer or printed cards. Free path: clean foam trays bought new for the purpose, tempera thickened with a little dish soap or glue for ink, a spoon to rub the print by hand. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 8 because a two-color edition is the band's hardest making technique and needs the most sessions; copyright labels come before units 10 and 11, where groups borrow images for their inquiry and zine | Candidate: a self-portrait by a living maker from Asia or Latin America beside the two portraits, described and linked | Portraits: no task asks about a student's own appearance, body, clothes or culture; the image a person projects is studied through the portraits and invented personas (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). The Obama portrait is studied as a painting; no political view is asked or marked. Samba's paintings often mock public life: the unit plan picks one with no adult content, and the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page first. Hazards and controls: water-based block ink labeled non-toxic only, no solvents; brayers rolled on a covered table; no blades; foam trays new or washed, never from raw meat; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
271: | 10 | 8 | Art of everyday life now. *How do artists today look into everyday life, and how do we judge a work still in progress and plan its changes?* | 14 | va-g8-ideas; va-g8-revise | VA:Cr1.1.8a; VA:Cr1.2.8a; VA:Cr3.1.8a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Leonardo da Vinci's notebook pages (Italy, about 1478 to 1518, the Codex Arundel, the British Library, digitized, public domain), a record of early steps in pictures and words; fabric versions of the homes he lived in by Do Ho Suh (born 1962 in Korea; the unit plan picks the work and records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to a museum's page; the class's own records | Retrieval in day 2's opener: strategies when stuck and written guidelines (va-g7-ideas) and a reflection in a chosen format (va-g7-revise). VA:Cr1.1.8a: record early creative steps in pictures or words, as they happen, in an old or new medium (sketchbook pages, a photo log of the pages on the school's device). VA:Cr1.2.8a: as a group, shape an inquiry into one part of present-day life using a current practice of art or design (for example, how people use a shared space at school, studied by observing, gesture drawing and mapping as artists who make work from daily life do), with each student's part on the plan. VA:Cr3.1.8a: apply fitting criteria to examine and think over the unfinished work, then plan changes in writing before making them. Technique: gesture and figure drawing: one-minute to five-minute gesture studies, then longer figure studies, with the figure about seven and a half heads tall as a rough guide (counted, no ratio), drawn from a jointed figure, from public pictures or from classmates who choose to pose, clothed. Words: record, process, inquiry, present-day, gesture, criteria. Showing: each group's inquiry works and records, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr1.1.8a, a guide look-for on the kept record: early steps recorded as they happened over at least three sessions, in pictures or words; VA:Cr1.2.8a, a kept card: the group's inquiry plan (the part of present-day life, the current practice used, the student's own part), one card a student; VA:Cr3.1.8a, a written item: a Curio Class original shown unfinished, criteria chosen and applied and two changes planned in writing (the student's own written revision plan from the critique day is carried unscored). Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: standards from other cultures for review (Level 1). Tools: gesture step cards, the record page, the group plan card and the revision plan sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil, charcoal or a marker, newsprint or scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 9 because the risk log becomes the record of early steps, and before unit 11 because the group inquiry is the class's first group work of the year and readies the group mural. Criteria for unfinished work build on grade 7's guidelines | Candidate: a living maker from Africa or Latin America whose practice studies everyday life, described and linked | Figure drawing: clothed only, posing is a choice, and no comment is made on anyone's body; observation at school records no faces and no names, and no photograph of a person is taken without the family's yes. Hazards and controls: charcoal dust as in unit 1 (review); no blades. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

