272: | 11 | 8 | Words, pictures and us. *How do pictures and words work together to carry a message, and how can art made together strengthen a group?* | 16 | va-g8-design; va-g8-judge | VA:Cr2.3.8a; VA:Cn10.1.8a; VA:Re8.1.8a; VA:Re9.1.8a | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Judith F. Baca, The Great Wall of Los Angeles (United States, begun 1974, in the Tujunga Wash flood channel, painted with hundreds of young people; the unit plan records whether Baca is living, as of its date), described and linked to the mural's own pages; screen prints of words and pictures by Corita Kent (United States, 1918 to 1986; the Corita Art Center's pages), described and linked; Wifredo Lam, The Jungle (Cuba, 1943, the Museum of Modern Art), described and linked, for the argued judgment | Retrieval in day 2's opener: visual organizing strategies (va-g7-design) and taste against established criteria (va-g7-judge). VA:Cr2.3.8a: pick, arrange and design pictures and text for a clear, compelling presentation: a zine spread or a three-poster series on the group inquiry of unit 10, with lettering and images on a grid. VA:Cn10.1.8a: with a group, make a paper-panel mural that reflects on and strengthens the good side of a group the class shares (the class, the school, a team or club, or an invented group); no student is asked to stand for their own ethnicity, faith or family. VA:Re8.1.8a: write an analysis of how subject, form, structure, media, approach and setting together build a work's message and mood (Lam's painting, then Baca's panels). VA:Re9.1.8a: argue for a judgment of a work with solid reasons: a claim, reasons, evidence from the work and an answer to an opposing view (ELA grade 8 unit 2's argument writing, see Cross-subject). Technique: pictures and words on a grid (hand lettering, type size and weight, image and text hierarchy) for the spread; panels joined by a shared grid and color plan for the mural. Words: layout, message, audience, group identity, claim, counterclaim. Showing: the mural and the zines, shown to the school. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr2.3.8a, a guide look-for on the zine spread or posters: pictures and text chosen and arranged so the message reads first and the audience is named; VA:Cn10.1.8a, a guide look-for on the student's mural panel and the group's note: the panel's part in the group's chosen positive theme, said in the note; VA:Re8.1.8a, a written item (first check day): an unseen work's message and mood from how its subject, form, media, approach and setting work together; VA:Re9.1.8a, a written item (second check day): an argued judgment of an unseen work, with a claim, two reasons, evidence and a counterclaim answered. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a team proposal for a real space (Level 1). Tools: layout step cards, the mural grid plan, the group note card and the argument frame, Curio Class's own. Free path: scrap paper panels taped together, a borrowed marker set. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 10 because the zine presents the group inquiry and the mural needs a group that has worked together; after ELA grade 8's argument unit. Hands pictures and words to unit 12's exhibition stories | Candidate: a group or community art project from Africa or Asia beside Baca's mural, described and linked | Group identity: the group is one the class shares or an invented one, never a student's ethnicity, faith or family, and the task strengthens what is good in it. Baca's mural tells California's history, including hard history (the forced removal of Japanese Americans, segregation); the unit picks panels, and the sensitivity agent reads the mural's own pages and Baca's published words first. Lam's painting draws on Afro-Cuban religious figures: the agent reads the museum's page first; it is studied by looking and never imitated. Corita Kent was a member of a religious order: said in one plain sentence. Hazards and controls: markers labeled non-toxic, no aerosol paint; long paper on tables or the floor, with a clear walkway. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
273: | 12 | 8 | The show and the group. *How can a show change what visitors think, and how does art stand for and build a group's identity?* | 12 | va-g8-show; va-g8-museum | VA:Pr4.1.8a; VA:Pr5.1.8a; VA:Pr6.1.8a; VA:Cn11.1.8a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11). Works: Nation to Nation: Treaties Between the United States and American Indian Nations, an exhibition at the Smithsonian's National Museum of the American Indian, Washington (opened 2014; its pages, as of the unit plan's date); a pole carved by Robert Davidson (Haida, born 1946; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date) and raised in Old Massett, Haida Gwaii, in 1969, the first pole raised there in many decades (the unit plan checks the account on the Haida Gwaii Museum's or the artist's own pages) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: methods scored against a checklist (va-g7-show) and setting shaping a reaction (va-g7-museum). VA:Pr4.1.8a: create criteria and use them to judge a group of works for a show (the class juries portfolio pieces into a theme). VA:Pr5.1.8a: with a group, ready and hang the themed show and write its exhibition stories (an introduction panel and section texts) for viewers. VA:Pr6.1.8a: examine why and how a show or collection can influence visitors' thoughts, beliefs and experiences (the treaty exhibition's choices; the visitors' notes left at the class show). VA:Cn11.1.8a: tell apart the ways art stands for, builds, strengthens and mirrors a group's identity (Davidson's pole, Baca's mural from unit 11, a team's emblem), in a sort. Technique: jurying with criteria, sequencing works, sight lines and spacing, and writing wall stories to a word limit. Words: jury, criteria, theme, exhibition story, influence, identity. Showing: the year-end themed show for families and the school. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.8a, a kept card: the student's criteria and the jury sheet applied to the group of works; VA:Pr5.1.8a, a guide look-for on the showing: the student's part of the hang and the exhibition story they wrote; VA:Pr6.1.8a, a written item: an unseen exhibition described on a Curio Class card, how its choices may influence a visitor's thoughts or beliefs; VA:Cn11.1.8a, a written item: four unseen works sorted by the way each serves a group's identity (stands for, builds, strengthens, mirrors), with a reason for two. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an exhibit's effect on society and politics (Level 1). Tools: the jury sheet, the story-writing card, the identity sort and readings, Curio Class's own. Free path: painter's tape, cereal-box mounts, hand-written labels. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in the band because the show is juried from the year's kept work and hands the curating and museum threads to Level 1; assessed by its own end-of-unit assessment and the showing, before the year-end check in week 32 | Candidate: a community museum or show from Africa, Asia or Oceania that tells a group's own story, beside the treaty exhibition | The Haida pole: crest figures belong to lineages; the pole is studied by looking only, and no student carves or draws a Haida crest; the sensitivity agent reads the Haida Gwaii Museum's and the artist's own published words first. The treaty exhibition: treaties made and broken are told in the museum's own words, read first by the agent. A student's work is shown outside the class only with the family's yes and under the student's chosen name. Hazards and controls: work hung with tape or clips by students on the floor, any hanging above shoulder height by an adult; no ladders for students. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
