: two research methods shown on the pages, the one chosen and why; VA:Cr3.1.5a, a guide look-for on the revised statement: three art words explaining two choices, and the change made after the critique. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a planned inquiry with sources (grade 6). Tools: research-method cards, the statement frame card, the critique-protocol card, Curio Class's own. Free path: cast-offs, tape, yarn, scrap fabric. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 9 because the sculpture uses the logged techniques and craft care; longest year 5 unit (18 days) because it drafts, critiques and revises two products, a work and a written statement. Before unit 11 because labeling places in art words and comparing standards need the statement's art words | Candidate: a merged-idea work by a living maker from Asia or Latin America, described and linked | Interviews: of a classmate or a school adult only, or an invented person from a card, marked the same; no family or personal facts are written down. Hazoumè's masks come from fuel-can trade in Benin: the unit tells the reuse and the setting, the masks are his own art, not ceremonial masks, and the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page and the artist's own published words first; no child wears a mask. Hazards and controls: cast-offs cleaned, no glass, no sharp metal, no food containers with allergen traces; blunt yarn needles counted out and in; any cardboard cut with a blade is cut by an adult; no hot glue. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
