e map or sketch set: places or objects that matter named, described and drawn; VA:Cn10.1.5a, a guide look-for on the made work: at least four art and design words used as labels, and one thing never noticed before shown; VA:Re8.1.5a, a written item: the ideas and mood of an unseen work from its form, structure, setting card, subject, visual elements and media; VA:Re9.1.5a, a written item: two unseen works from different styles or cultures, each with a setting card, and one way the standards used to judge each differ. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: designing for named users (grade 6). Tools: the "made for" card, a symmetry fold card, setting cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencils, scrap paper, a folded paper square. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 10 because labeling in art and design words and comparing standards use the statement's art words; before unit 12 because a curator's argument and an exhibit's evidence need standards and settings already compared | Candidate: a fourth work judged by different standards, from Africa or the Americas, described and linked | Places that matter: invited, never required, with a public or invented place marked the same; no home address or route is drawn. Iznik tiles were made for mosques, palaces and tombs: the unit tells the setting, studies the pattern by looking, and the sensitivity agent reads the Met's page first. Hazards and controls: pencils and colored pencils only; the walk to look for pattern and symmetry stays on school grounds. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
272: | 12 | 5 | Curators and change. *What does a curator do, how does an exhibit teach about a topic, and how can art change what people believe or do?* | 12 | va-g5-show; va-g5-museum | VA:Pr4.1.5a; VA:Pr5.1.5a; VA:Pr6.1.5a; VA:Cn11.1.5a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11). Works: the exhibition Nation to Nation: Treaties Between the United States and American Indian Nations (the Smithsonian's National Museum of the American Indian, Washington, opened 2014; its own online pages), studied for how objects, labels and layout present a topic; Lewis Hine's photographs of child workers (United States, 1908 to 1924, the Library of Congress, no known restrictions), photographs that helped change laws; Maya Lin, the Vietnam Veterans Memorial (Washington, 1982; the artist born 1959; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked, a memorial that changed how people remember | Retrieval in day 2's opener: a plan for two places and formats (va-g4-show) and setting inferred from clues (va-g4-museum). VA:Pr4.1.5a: define a curator's roles and responsibilities and the skills and knowledge needed to preserve, maintain and present objects, artifacts and artwork (handling with clean hands, light and damp, condition notes, labels). VA:Pr5.1.5a: argue for safe, effective materials and techniques for preparing and showing the class's work (mounts, photo corners or glue stick against tape and pins), with two reasons and one risk avoided. VA:Pr6.1.5a: cite evidence from the exhibition's pages (an object, a label, the order of rooms) of how it presents ideas and information on its topic. VA:Cn11.1.5a: describe how art can change what a person or a society believes, values or does (Hine's photographs and child labor laws; Lin's memorial and the names on it). Words: curator, preserve, evidence, exhibit, argue, change. Showing: the year-end curated show of the year's work, with the artist statements, for families; children take the curator's jobs, with the family's yes for showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.5a, a written item: three curator jobs (preserve, maintain, present) and the skill or knowledge each needs; VA:Pr5.1.5a, a kept argument card made on the last making day: the chosen materials and mounting, two reasons and one risk avoided; VA:Pr6.1.5a, a written item: two pieces of evidence from a printed exhibit page set the class has not studied, and how each presents the topic; VA:Cn11.1.5a, a written item: how one of the works changed what people believed, valued or did. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: judging a museum's view of its community (grade 6). Tools: curator job cards, the argument card, printed exhibit page sets, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts, photo corners cut from scrap card. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in the band because the curated show is built from the year's kept work and statements and ends the year after the year-end check (week 26); the unit's showing in weeks 27 to 32 is its own assessment. The museum thread then goes to grade 6's judged museum | Candidate: a second exhibit's pages from outside the United States for the unseen evidence item | Hard history: the treaties exhibit tells of agreements made and broken; the unit says so plainly, studies how the exhibit is built, and has no role play; the sensitivity agent reads the museum's own pages first, written with the Native nations. Hine's photographs show children at hard and dangerous work: the unit picks photographs without injury, and the agent reads the Library of Congress pages first. The memorial lists the names of the dead: the unit tells its design and its visitors, not the war's battles, and a child with family loss may take the Hine photographs instead. Health's steps if a child tells something. Glue sticks; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

