1: # Course frame: Visual Arts, grades 9 to 12
2: 
3: Course `visual-arts-grades-9-to-12` (catalog id `visual-arts-9-12`), a band course for high school, run as four levels one after another (Level 1, Level 2, Level 3 and the optional Level 4), each a full year, named by level and never by grade (STANDARD.md 3.2). Draft frame, written 2026-11-25; the scope and sequence sets the days later, one level at a time, and each level's part of this file is that level's frame.
4: 
5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 19: five in each of Levels 1, 2 and 3, four in Level 4. Levels 1 to 3 share one shape, so a thread
8:   comes back one level harder in the same place: an opening unit (Level 1's bridge, the review unit in Levels 2 and
9:   3), then a looking and materials unit, an ideas and revision unit, a design and judging unit, and a curating and
10:   museum unit that ends the year with the level's show. Each of the four paired units joins two progression threads,
11:   one of creating and one of responding, presenting or connecting, so every unit makes, studies other artists' work
12:   and shows (bar criteria 4 and 5). Level 4 is one sustained body of work in four units, one for each Level 4 row:
13:   the proposal and series, the critical essay, the project with a purpose, and the exhibit (APPROACH "High school
14:   levels and music").
15: - **Days.** 640: 160 a level for four levels (Level 4 optional; a school that offers three levels uses 480). Each
16:   level: 156 days in its units and 4 for course-level checks and respond days (a mid-year check and a year-end check,
17:   each with one respond day). One lesson of 45 minutes a day, five a week, 32 weeks a level (STANDARD.md 3.4: a high
18:   school level is 160 course days of 45 minutes, the about 120 hours the national Proficient descriptor names; the
19:   80-day short path is 60 hours). The 45 minutes hold the set-up routine (about 3 minutes), the teaching and making,
20:   and clean-up and collecting work (about 7 minutes in an ink, paint, clay or printing session, 3 in a drawing,
21:   writing or talk session); a retrieval task takes ten minutes. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment
22:   and, in units over 25 days, its mid-unit assessment, each with a respond day. Unit counts follow the rows' load:
23:   the design and judging unit is longest in Levels 1 to 3 because it holds a team or solo build with a written
24:   rationale and a written judgment; the bridge and review units are short because they own no id. These are course
25:   days: the school calendar (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) runs them one a school day, so course day n of a level falls
26:   in that level's week n / 5, rounded up. National time figure: the visual arts educators' association's high school
27:   checklist has not been read at source, so no association figure is stated here (STANDARD.md 3.4: a figure not read
28:   at source stays off the page; For the lead item 5).
29: - **The 80-day short path (a semester or a rotation).** Each level's scope and sequence marks an 80-day path that
30:   keeps every unit and every id by merging the marked days (STANDARD.md 3.2). Days on that path: Level 1, units of
31:   6 (the bridge), 15, 16, 20 and 19; Levels 2 and 3, units of 3 (review), 16, 17, 20 and 20; Level 4, units of 25,
32:   14, 19 and 18; each level's two checks keep 1 check day and 1 respond day. Checks fall in week 8 (mid-course) and
33:   week 16, the half-year finals week. Each unit keeps, in this order and no fewer: the demonstration with a step card,
34:   opened by the look at a work that carries the unit's response and its connecting line; the making days with the
35:   one critique and the revision; the mid-unit and end-of-unit assessments; the respond days. Fluency days fold into
36:   the openers, and the two rows' understanding days merge where the scope marks them. A merged day never drops a
37:   critique, a revision or a respond day. The bridge's 6 short-path days are 7.5 percent of 80, inside the bar's 10
38:   percent limit on days outside the course's standards (12 of 160 on the full path, the same share).
39: - **Levels, entry and placement.** STANDARD.md 3.2 binds. Level 1 is Proficient (`.Ia` ids), Level 2 Accomplished
40:   (`.IIa`), Level 3 Advanced at honors depth (`.IIIa`), Level 4 an optional year the student leads, assessed again
41:   on `.IIIa` with new, self-directed work. A student new to visual arts starts at Level 1 in any grade. Each level
42:   needs the one before, or a placement showing: the student's work scored against the previous level's year-end
43:   check, met at the review unit (units 6 and 11). Advanced is claimed by assessed work, not by finishing Level 3; a
44:   student who ends Level 3 at "partly" has Level 4 to reach it. One level is a complete course. Choice grows by level
45:   (APPROACH): Level 1 sets the problems, Level 2 lets the student pick among them and research, Level 3 has the
46:   student set the problem, Level 4 is the student's own proposal.
47: - **Standard.** National Core Arts Standards, 2014, visual arts performance standards, by id only, each named with
48:   its line in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt, never quoted (STANDARD.md 2); each id's action
49:   was checked against the national standard's own text for this frame. Level 1 claims the 15 `.Ia` ids, Level 2 the
50:   15 `.IIa` ids, Level 3 the 15 `.IIIa` ids: 45 in all, the count in STANDARD.md 3.2, every one taught and assessed in
51:   its own level. Level 4 claims no new id: its rows contribute to the `.IIIa` ids, which it assesses again on new work.
52:   No grade 8 id is claimed (the bridge teaches grade 8 techniques and words as readiness). Every level touches all
53:   four artistic processes (creating, presenting, responding, connecting; bar criterion 1), and each unit touches at
54:   least two. In the Units table the seventh column is headed "Processes, anchors and works". Check set: each unit
55:   plan checks itself against the words-file lines of its ids, the nine criteria of the arts part of
56:   docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md and the lens's error list (docs/agents/lenses/arts.md); the "Check set adds" column
57:   names what that is expected to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds: on political and social works,
58:   facts and backed sources decide claims, people are taught with dignity, and a student's own view is never scored,
59:   only the evidence and reasoning behind it. scripts/standards.py checks every id against the id file; each level's
60:   scope and sequence still states its id count (15) against STANDARD.md 3.2.
61: - **Base text.** None. Visual arts is Curio Class's own (docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md; the open-material scout,
62:   docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md, found material to study, not plans to follow). Works studied come
63:   from museum open-access collections and public domain government collections (public domain works released by the
64:   holding museum or library) and, for living or recent makers, from a description in our own words with a link to
65:   the maker's or museum's page, never a copy (APPROACH "Rights"). Each work named below is a pick the unit plan keeps
66:   unless its rights line, checked on the item's own page, fails; a swapped work keeps the unit's ids met and the
67:   level's range. Across the band the works come from Africa, the Americas (with four Native American makers or
68:   Native-led shows: units 5, 9, 13, 18 and 19), Asia, Europe and Oceania, from about 420 BCE to now, with living
69:   makers in every level (APPROACH "Works and their settings"); open images lean to older European and American works,
70:   so the list was built for range. Curio Class writes every step card, work card, hazard card, reading, case card,
71:   record sheet, guide and family note.
72: - **Techniques that get harder (bar criterion 2).** Drawing: value scales, contour and gesture, one-point
73:   perspective (the bridge, as grade 8 readiness), then thumbnails and observational studies (Level 1), sustained
74:   studies over a term (Level 2), a planned series (Level 3), a self-set series (Level 4). Wet media: ink and
75:   watercolor worked without a plan (Level 1), mixed media weighed old and new (Level 2), a series in one chosen
76:   medium (Level 3). Form and space: a scale model of a school space in card (Level 1), a redesigned object or place
77:   model (Level 2), a work about material culture (Level 3). Printmaking: foam-plate and collagraph prints, no blade
78:   cutting (Levels 1 and 2). Presenting: a small show and a kept collection (Level 1), a sited portfolio show with
79:   hanging and lighting chosen (Level 2), a curated event show with justified choices and a conservation plan (Level
80:   3), a public exhibit with labels and a talk (Level 4).
81: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md ("teaching day") and APPROACH "Kinds of day". Understanding: a technique shown
82:   in named steps with the step card, or a work studied with the unit's words (the artistic process "responding" is
83:   taught here, never on a respond day); first teaching is always understanding. Fluency: short repeated drills with
84:   feedback (a two-minute gesture set, a value strip, a thumbnail page, a mixing chart, a looking routine on a new
85:   image); in high school it is the five-minute opener on most days and five or six fluency days a unit. Application:
86:   making and writing with some choice, drafting and editing included; the second making week holds the unit's one
87:   critique (describe, ask, suggest), and the revision days after it are application too. Check: the mid-unit and
88:   end-of-unit assessments and the course-level checks. Respond: a second try at the part marked "not yet". A paired
89:   unit of 32 to 40 days runs roughly: 9 understanding, 6 fluency, 14 to 20 application with the critique and the
90:   revision, 1 mid-unit check and 1 respond, 2 end check days, 1 respond.
91: - **Scored evidence.** Every owned id has an end-of-unit item or a look-for on the unit's guide, named id by id in
92:   the unit's "End-of-unit evidence" line. A progression row's "Assessed by" note names one form for the row, not one
93:   item for each id, so it is never the whole line. Items come in four kinds: a look-for on the product's guide (three
94:   to five look-fors, met, partly or not yet), scored from the kept work after the session; a kept page made on the
95:   unit's making days (a planning portfolio entry, a hazard card, an idea log, a research page), scored with the
96:   product; a written item in a sitting on an unseen open-licensed work or a Curio Class original; and the artist
97:   statement or written rationale, drafted, given written feedback and revised before it is scored. Each paired unit
98:   has two end check days: day one is a sitting of written items on unseen works (set-up 3 minutes, 38 minutes of
99:   writing, collecting 4); day two is the showing, where each student self-scores on the guide and the class gives
100:   the showing's audience notes, and the product is scored from the kept work afterwards. A check day has no paint,
101:   ink or clay, and one adult watches one room. The product's draft, its critique with the teacher's written marks on
102:   the record sheet, and its revision come on earlier days counted in the unit. No score comes from the day a technique
103:   or a work is first taught. The mid-unit assessment (one check day and one respond day inside every paired unit)
104:   scores the unit's planning or materials item while the product is still in progress. Each unit opens with a
105:   baseline look at its main look-for during the first practice days (APPROACH "Growth"), so every student has two
106:   marks a unit and the growth is the change. One piece a unit goes into the portfolio with a one-line written
107:   reflection (bar criterion 8); the portfolio is the Level 2 portfolio show's source and Level 4's starting point.
108: - **Readiness and retrieval.** Level 1 assumes no earlier arts learning (STANDARD.md 3.2). Its unit 1, the bridge,
109:   teaches the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs to every student, as readiness, and claims no grade 8 id; it
110:   stands in for a readiness check, since the grade 8 rows were last used one grade back for a student who took Visual
111:   Arts, grades 6 to 8 straight through, and two or more grades back (or never) for a student who starts later. Units
112:   2 to 5 then open with a retrieval task inside day 2's opener on the bridge's technique and the grade 8 row in the
113:   same thread (taught the same year in the bridge), and day 1 stays clean for the hook and the question. Levels 2, 3
114:   and 4 assume the level before, taken the year before (the count starts from the band's order: a student who takes
115:   one level a year). Their units open with a retrieval task inside day 2 on the level before's row in the same thread.
116:   A student who placed in, or skipped a year between levels, meets the level before's techniques again in the review
117:   unit (units 6 and 11), whose placement sitting stands as the readiness check; Level 4 has no review unit, so a
118:   student returning after a gap takes unit 16's first checkpoint as its readiness check (its ten minutes on the last
119:   day of unit 15's respond work for a student continuing). The Seams tables give each row's last use.
120: - **Course-level checks.** Grades 9 to 12 have one rule: week 32 is the finals week (CALENDAR.md stagger rule 4).
121:   Each level's mid-year check is in week 16, at the semester's end (course days 79 and 80 in Level 1, 77 and 78 in
122:   Levels 2 and 3, 79 and 80 in Level 4); its year-end check is in week 32, the finals week (days 159 and 160). The
123:   curating unit's show, in weeks 25 to 31, is its own end-of-unit assessment, and the year-end check samples the
124:   units before it.
125: - **Grade 12 calendar.** Any level may be taken in grade 12 (Levels 3 and 4 most often). Its last weeks hold
126:   teaching: the curating or exhibit unit runs to week 31 and the year-end check sits in week 32. In a school whose
127:   seniors leave before week 32, a senior sits the year-end check on the school's senior exam day (week 30 or 31), and
128:   the curating unit's last fluency days and two of its making days fold (up to five days, marked in the scope); the
129:   show, its critique, revision and respond days never fold. A Level 4 senior's exhibit opens by week 30 for the same
130:   reason. A student of 18 or over gets the family note themselves and gives their own yes to be shown (AGENTS.md;
131:   va-g12-exhibit); the school's own rules still apply.
132: - **Leave-out.** A school or family may leave out a unit or a work for its community's reasons (STANDARD.md 5); each
133:   unit stands alone. Units this course names: unit 15 (it studies Benin royal altar works, a ceremonial use, by
134:   looking only), unit 14 (war and colonial imagery: Guernica and the Dufour wallpaper) and unit 5 (a museum show built
135:   around objects of slavery). Swapping a work keeps the unit's ids met; leaving a unit out means the course page and
136:   the unit's family note name the ids not claimed that level.
137: - **The ordinary room.** A class of 30 at tables, paper, pencils, charcoal, found material; nothing must be bought,
138:   and every unit names a free stand-in (STANDARD.md 3.4, APPROACH "Supplies"). One student at home: the parent or a
139:   sibling is the audience and the critique partner, a team task has a solo version with the same look-fors, and the
140:   showing is to the family. The teacher needs no art training: each unit's teacher page says what to look for, in
257: | 1 | Level 1 | The bridge: tools of seeing. *What does an artist need to know before the work begins?* | 12 | | None | Creating (Cr2, practiced as readiness); responding (Re7, practiced). Works: Elizabeth Catlett, Sharecropper (United States and Mexico, 1952, linocut; the unit plan records the holding museum's page and rights line), value built from line; Vincent van Gogh, The Bedroom (France, 1889, the Art Institute of Chicago, open access), color and a tilted perspective | Readiness, claiming no id (STANDARD.md 3.2): the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs, taught to every student. A value scale in pencil and charcoal; contour and gesture drawing; one-point perspective from a school hallway; mixing a color wheel and its neutrals; the sketchbook and practice log set up; the hazard card routine and clean-up; the critique routine (describe, ask, suggest); labeling a borrowed image as own, open license or public domain, with credit (the grade 8 craft row's idea, reviewed). The looking routine (look, describe, interpret) on the two works, each with a work card. Words: value, contour, gesture, perspective, credit, critique. Showing: a value study and a perspective drawing on the class wall. Evidence: no score; a readiness record of each technique (met, partly, not yet) sets unit 2's groups and its reteach, and the baseline looks start here. Out: every Level 1 id (units 2 to 5). Tools: step cards, the hazard card, work cards, a credit-label card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil for every value study (charcoal optional, from a school set), copy-paper sketchbook. Short path: 6 days | First because Level 1 assumes no earlier arts learning and every later unit needs these techniques, the studio routine and the looking routine; it stands in for a readiness check (Readiness and retrieval). At most 12 days, inside the bar's 10 percent limit | Candidate: a value study by a maker from Asia or Africa beside Catlett's, for range | Hazards and controls: charcoal dust wiped damp, never blown; hands washed; pencils and sharpeners only, no blades. Catlett's print shows a farm worker: the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and Catlett's own published words first. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
258: | 2 | Level 1 | How images shape us. *How do the images we see shape how we understand people and the world, and what does a material do when we make without a plan?* | 32 | va-g9-look; va-g9-craft | VA:Re7.1.Ia; VA:Re7.2.Ia; VA:Cr2.1.Ia; VA:Cr2.2.Ia | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2); connecting touched through the works' settings. Works: Jacob Lawrence, The Migration Series (United States, 1940 to 1941, 60 panels held by The Phillips Collection and The Museum of Modern Art), described and linked; Helen Frankenthaler, Mountains and Sea (United States, 1952, on loan to the National Gallery of Art), poured and soaked paint, described and linked; Ibrahim El-Salahi, Reborn Sounds of Childhood Dreams I (Sudan, 1961 to 1965, Tate; born 1930, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), forms grown from calligraphic marks, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the bridge's looking routine and the grade 8 look and craft rows (va-g8-look, va-g8-craft). VA:Re7.1.Ia: write a short proposal on how one set of works (the Migration Series) shapes how people understand a human experience. VA:Re7.2.Ia: analyze, in a written reflection, how images seen every day (a feed, an ad, a poster the student picks, printed or described) shape the student's own view of the world. VA:Cr2.1.Ia: make a work in ink and watercolor, or in moist air-dry clay, without planning it first, and log what the material did. VA:Cr2.2.Ia: explain how traditional and non-traditional materials (pigments, inks, clay, found plastics) can affect people's health and nature, from hazard cards, and handle them safely. Words: perceive, experience, analyze, unplanned, hazard, ventilation. Showing: the unplanned works with their log pages, on the class wall. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Re7.1.Ia, the kept written proposal, scored on its guide (a claim about understanding, evidence from three panels, their setting); VA:Re7.2.Ia, a sitting item: an unseen open-licensed image, how seeing it could shape a viewer's view of the world, with the student's own case; VA:Cr2.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the unplanned work and its practice log: worked with no plan, what the material did named twice; VA:Cr2.2.Ia, a sitting item: from an unseen hazard card, one health and one nature effect and the safe handling steps, and a guide look-for on handling observed on the rotating list during the making days. Mid-unit assessment (day 16): the hazard card item for the unit's two materials, with a respond day. Out: judging how well images sway a named audience (Level 2). Tools: work cards, hazard cards, a reading on the Great Migration's art, a practice log page, Curio Class's own; a work viewer or printed cards. Free path: tea or coffee as a wash, pencil, scrap paper. Short path: 15 days | First paired unit because every later unit needs the safe-studio habits of this level (hazard cards before a material) and the looking routine applied to a set of works; unplanned making comes before the planned starts of unit 3 so students know what materials do | Candidate: an unplanned or process-led work by a living maker from Asia or the Americas | Hazards and controls: ink and watercolor on tables (covered tables, water cups not near hands); air-dry clay moist, not sanded; hands washed; ventilation on for ink. The Migration Series tells of segregation and violence in the American South: the sensitivity agent reads the Phillips Collection's and MoMA's pages and Lawrence's own captions first; panels showing violence are named and studied by choice. The everyday-images reflection uses images the student chooses, never a feed handed in, and no student's own photos of people. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
259: | 3 | Level 1 | Starting points. *How do artists start, and whose standards should we use to judge unfinished work?* | 34 | va-g9-ideas; va-g9-revise | VA:Cr1.1.Ia; VA:Cr1.2.Ia; VA:Cr3.1.Ia | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review); connecting through the works' sources. Works: Wangechi Mutu, The NewOnes, will free Us (Kenya-born, works in the United States and Kenya; 2019, four bronze figures for the Metropolitan Museum of Art's facade; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; a caryatid from the Erechtheion, Athens (Greece, about 421 to 406 BCE, the British Museum), the old figure Mutu answers; Xie He's six principles of painting (China, about 500 CE), a past culture's named standards, given in our own summary | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the grade 8 ideas and revise rows (va-g8-ideas, va-g8-revise) and the bridge's critique routine. VA:Cr1.1.Ia: start one project three ways (a word web, a thumbnail page, a photo walk or viewfinder walk) and pick the strongest with a reason. VA:Cr1.2.Ia: frame a question about daily life today that a current artist or designer has also taken up, and shape the project around it. VA:Cr3.1.Ia: review and plan revisions of the unfinished work with standards from a past culture (Xie He's principles) and a present one (the class's own guide, or a living maker's stated aims), naming whose standards each is. Technique: thumbnails and a sustained observational study in pencil and ink. The first artist statement of the level, drafted, given written feedback and revised. Words: route, inquiry, contemporary, practice, standard, revise. Showing: the finished work beside its three starts and its revision plan, for another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr1.1.Ia, a guide look-for on the planning portfolio entry: three start routes tried, the strongest chosen with a reason; VA:Cr1.2.Ia, a guide look-for on the same entry and the work: a question about daily life today, tied to a named current maker's practice; VA:Cr3.1.Ia, the critique record and the revised work, scored on the guide: two standards named as whose, a planned change, the change made, and a sitting item applying one named standard to an unseen unfinished work (a Curio Class original). Mid-unit assessment (day 17): the planning portfolio entry, with a respond day. Out: problems drawn from the student's own earlier work (Level 2). Tools: step cards, a summary card of Xie He's principles, work cards, the critique card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil, ink from a borrowed set, copy-paper sketchbook. Short path: 16 days | After unit 2 because a planned start needs what unplanned work showed about materials, and the revision uses the critique routine on a work in progress. Before unit 4 because the team proposal starts from this unit's start routes and idea pages | Candidate: a present-day standards set from outside Europe and North America, from its own published source | Mutu's figures answer caryatids, which hold up a building: the unit names the old use and her stated aims, from the Met's pages, read first by the sensitivity agent. Photo walk: no faces of people without their yes, school grounds only, the class together. Hazards and controls: ink on tables (covered), hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
