he idea log: at least four dated steps from first spark to the final proposal, with what changed at each; VA:Re8.1.Ia, a sitting item: an interpretation of an unseen open-licensed work with three pieces of evidence from the work and one from its setting; VA:Re9.1.Ia, a sitting item: three standards set for judging that work, each with a reason it fits. Mid-unit assessment (day 20): the idea log to date and the first plan drawing, with a respond day. Out: redesign in answer to an issue (Level 2). Tools: a design-brief card, a scale ruler card, work cards, a reading on the memorial competition, Curio Class's own; an exhibit floor-plan tool or its printed grid. Free path: cereal-box card, tape, pencil. Short path: 20 days | After unit 3 because the proposal starts from the start routes and the idea log grows from the planning pages; judging needs unit 2's looking routine and adds evidence and standards. Before unit 5 because curating a show uses this unit's standards for a collection. Longest Level 1 unit: a team build, a log, a rationale and two written items | Candidate: a team-design case from Asia or Latin America (a public space changed by design), from an open source | Proposals are drawings and models; nothing is built or changed on school grounds without permission (va-g9-design note). The memorial lists the names of the dead of the Vietnam War: the unit tells the design and its jury, the sensitivity agent reads the National Park Service's pages first, and a student with a family tie may choose another work. Team work: each student's part is named on the log, and a solo version exists. Hazards and controls: card and tape only, no blades (an adult cuts any thick card), glue sticks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
