res recorded a story or history; VA:Cn11.1.3a, a written item: a first reaction to an unseen work, then the reaction after its setting card, and what changed. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: technology for keeping art (grade 4). Tools: the show-space card, artist-note card, work and setting cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in year 3 because the show is chosen from the year's kept work (units 1 to 3) and ends the year after the year-end check (week 26); the unit's showing in weeks 27 to 32 is its own assessment. The exhibit-plan and work-card models move to grade 3's step here | Candidate: a story-telling work from Asia or Africa beside the three, by a living maker | Hard history: the Great Migration was a flight from violence and unfair laws; the unit picks panels of travel, work and new homes, and leaves out the panels that show violence; the winter count may record war, sickness or hunger years, named in one plain sentence; the Bayeux embroidery shows a battle, shown in the panels of building, sailing and feasting. The sensitivity agent reads the Phillips Collection's pages on the series, the Smithsonian's pages on winter counts with Lakota keepers' own words, and the Bayeux Museum's page first. The class web page is the school's own and only the teacher posts to it, under each child's chosen name with the family's yes. Glue sticks; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
